            0.043
          ],
          "content": "Chapter 5 — Exploring Forces"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.892,
            0.03,
            0.917,
            0.045
          ],
          "content": "69"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.057,
            0.339,
            0.162
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.345,
            0.089,
            0.637,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": "Is it essential for an object applying force on another object to always be in contact with it?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.173,
            0.397,
            0.192
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.4.2 Non-contact forces"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.196,
            0.744,
            0.253
          ],
          "content": "There are forces whose effect can be experienced even if the objects are not in contact. These forces are called **non-contact forces**. Let us learn about non-contact forces."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.263,
            0.294,
            0.283
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Magnetic force"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.286,
            0.744,
            0.491
          ],
          "content": "Do you remember learning about magnets in the chapter ‘Exploring Magnets’ in Curiosity, Grade 6? We learnt that a magnet attracts objects made of magnetic materials. When two magnets are brought close to each other, like poles (North–North, South–South) repel each other while unlike poles (North–South) attract each other. In an earlier chapter of this book, we also learnt about electromagnets which behave like magnets. Attraction and repulsion between objects are also a form of push and pull, that is, a force. Can you recall that a magnet could exert force on another magnet or a magnetic material without being in contact with it?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.508,
            0.436,
            0.532
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 5.5: Let us test"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.551,
            0.579,
            0.571
          ],
          "content": "z Take two ring magnets and a wooden stick."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.575,
            0.742,
            0.614
          ],
          "content": "z While holding the stick in a vertical position over a wooden table, insert one ring magnet onto the stick (Fig. 5.7)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.618,
            0.743,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": "z Now insert the second ring magnet above it such that the like poles of the two magnets face each other. Does the second magnet stay floating above the first magnet?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.678,
            0.743,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "z Try pushing the second magnet down gently. Do you feel a force on it?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.721,
            0.742,
            0.76
          ],
          "content": "z Now, reverse the poles of both the magnets. Does the second magnet still remain floating?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.793,
            0.596,
            0.894,
            0.734
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.757,
                0.74,
                0.941,
                0.773
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.7: Force between two ring magnets"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.764,
            0.743,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": "We find that a magnet can exert force on another magnet without being in contact with it."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.804,
            0.744,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": "The force exerted by a magnet on another magnet or a magnetic material is called **magnetic force**. Since a magnet can exert a force from a distance without being in contact it is called a non-contact force."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.879,
            0.67,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": "Are there more such forces which act from a distance?"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
